"quotes": [{"text": "Showing the population what we wish them to be is the best way for them to change.", "reference": "2018 May 13, Justin King, “How to Fix the Storylines of Film and Television”, in Return of Kings:"}, {"text": "I would not wish / Any companion in the world but you.", "reference": "1610–1611 (date written), William Shakespeare, “The Tempest”, in Mr. William Shakespeares Comedies, Histories, & Tragedies […] (First Folio), London: […] Isaac Iaggard, and Ed[ward] Blount, published 1623, →OCLC, [Act III, scene i]:"}, {"text": "Now John the butler must be sent\nTo learn the road that Phyllis went:\nThe groom was wished to saddle Crop;\nFor John must neither light nor stop,\nBut find her, wheresoe'er she fled,\nAnd bring her back alive or dead.", "reference": "1716, Jonathan Swift, Phyllis, or the Progress of Love:"}, {"text": "Yesterday, upon the stair / I met a man who wasn’t there / He wasn’t there again today / I wish, I wish he’d go away …", "reference": "1899, Hughes Mearns, Antigonish:"}], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": []}, {"definition": "(transitive, now rare) To hope (+ object clause with may or in present subjunctive).", "tags": ["archaic", "transitive"], "examples": ["She hears that Miss Bigg is to be married in a fortnight. I wish it may be so."], "quotes": [{"text": "I wish he mean me well, that he takes so much pains!", "reference": "1748, [Samuel Richardson], “Letter LXXXIV”, in Clarissa. Or, The History of a Young Lady: […], volume (please specify |volume=I to VII), London: […] S[amuel] Richardson; […], →OCLC:"}], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": []}, {"definition": "(intransitive, followed by for) To hope (for a particular outcome), even if that outcome is unlikely to occur or cannot occur.", "tags": ["intransitive"], "examples": ["I wish I could go back in time and teach myself what I know now.", "I wished (that) it would rain."], "quotes": [{"text": "This is as good an argument as an antiquary could wish for.", "reference": "1727, John Arbuthnot, Tables of Ancient Coins, Weights and Measures:"}, {"text": "Mr. White took the paw from his pocket and eyed it dubiously. \"I don't know what to wish for, and that's a fact,\" he said slowly. \"It seems to me I've got all I want.\"", "reference": "1901, W. W. Jacobs, The Monkey's Paw:"}], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": []}, {"definition": "(ditransitive) To bestow (a thought or gesture) towards (someone or something).", "tags": ["ditransitive"], "examples": ["We wish you a merry Christmas."], "quotes": [{"text": "I would not wish them to a fairer death.", "reference": "c. 1606 (date written), William Shakespeare, “The Tragedie of Macbeth”, in Mr. William Shakespeares Comedies, Histories, & Tragedies […] (First Folio), London: […] Isaac Iaggard, and Ed[ward] Blount, published 1623, →OCLC, [Act V, scene viii]:"}, {"text": "Let them be driven backward, and put to shame, that wish me evil.", "reference": "1611, The Holy Bible, […] (King James Version), London: […] Robert Barker, […], →OCLC, Psalms 40:14:"}], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": [], "translations": [{"language": {"code": "az", "name": "Azerbaijani"}, "word": "diləmək"}, {"language": {"code": "bg", "name": "Bulgarian"}, "word": "жела́я"}, {"language": {"code": "ca", "name": "Catalan"}, "word": "desitjar"}, {"language": {"code": "cic", "name": "Chickasaw"}, "word": "anhi"}, {"language": {"code": "cic", "name": "Chickasaw"}, "word": "imanhi"}, {"language": {"code": "cmn", "name": "Chinese Mandarin"}, "word": "祝"}, {"language": {"code": "nl", "name": "Dutch"}, "word": "wensen"}, {"language": {"code": "eo", "name": "Esperanto"}, "word": "deziri"}, {"language": {"code": "fo", "name": "Faroese"}, "word": "ynskja"}, {"language": {"code": "fi", "name": "Finnish"}, "word": "toivottaa"}, {"language": {"code": "fr", "name": "French"}, "word": "souhaiter"}, {"language": {"code": "gl", "name": "Galician"}, "word": "desexar a"}, {"language": {"code": "de", "name": "German"}, "word": "wünschen"}, {"language": {"code": "hu", "name": "Hungarian"}, "word": "kíván"}, {"language": {"code": "it", "name": "Italian"}, "word": "augurare"}, {"language": {"code": "km", "name": "Khmer"}, "word": "សូមជូនពរ"}, {"language": {"code": "mk", "name": "Macedonian"}, "word": "посакува"}, {"language": {"code": "mk", "name": "Macedonian"}, "word": "посака"}, {"language": {"code": "fa", "name": "Persian"}, "word": "آرزو کردن"}, {"language": {"code": "fa", "name": "Persian"}, "word": "آرزو داشتن"}, {"language": {"code": "pl", "name": "Polish"}, "word": "życzyć"}, {"language": {"code": "pl", "name": "Polish"}, "word": "zażyczyć"}, {"language": {"code": "pt", "name": "Portuguese"}, "word": "desejar a"}, {"language": {"code": "ro", "name": "Romanian"}, "word": "ura"}, {"language": {"code": "gd", "name": "Scottish Gaelic"}, "word": "guidh"}, {"language": {"code": "es", "name": "Spanish"}, "word": "desear"}, {"language": {"code": "sv", "name": "Swedish"}, "word": "tillönska"}, {"language": {"code": "ta", "name": "Tamil"}, "word": "வாழ்த்து"}, {"language": {"code": "tyz", "name": "Tày"}, "word": "ái"}, {"language": {"code": "uk", "name": "Ukrainian"}, "word": "зи́чити"}, {"language": {"code": "uk", "name": "Ukrainian"}, "word": "бажа́ти"}, {"language": {"code": "uk", "name": "Ukrainian"}, "word": "побажа́ти"}, {"language": {"code": "vi", "name": "Vietnamese"}, "word": "chúc"}], "subsenses": []}, {"definition": "(intransitive, followed by to and an infinitive) To request or desire to do an activity.", "tags": ["intransitive"], "examples": ["Anyone who wishes to may leave now."], "quotes": [{"text": "Energy has seldom been found where we need it when we want it. Ancient nomads, wishing to ward off the evening chill and enjoy a meal around a campfire, had to collect wood and then spend time and effort coaxing the heat of friction out from between sticks to kindle a flame. With more settled people, animals were harnessed to capstans or caged in treadmills to turn grist into meal.", "reference": "2013 July-August, Henry Petroski, “Geothermal Energy”, in American Scientist, volume 101, number 4:"}], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": []}, {"definition": "(transitive) To recommend; to seek confidence or favour on behalf of.", "tags": ["transitive"], "examples": [], "quotes": [{"text": "I was wished to your worship by a gentleman.", "reference": "1610 (first performance), Ben[jamin] Jonson, The Alchemist, London: […] Thomas Snodham, for Walter Burre, and are to be sold by Iohn Stepneth, […], published 1612, →OCLC, (please specify the Internet Archive page), (please specify the scene number in lowercase Roman numerals):"}, {"text": "Farewell: yet for the loue I beare my sweet Bianca, if I can by any meanes light on a fit man to teach her that wherein she delights, I will wish him to her father.", "reference": "c. 1590–1592 (date written), William Shakespeare, “The Taming of the Shrew”, in Mr. William Shakespeares Comedies, Histories, & Tragedies […] (First Folio), London: […] Isaac Iaggard, and Ed[ward] Blount, published 1623, →OCLC, [Act I, scene i]:"}], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": []}], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": []}], "source": {"url": "https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/wish", "license": {"name": "CC BY-SA 4.0", "url": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/"}}}}